.TH GERANT\-NET 8 2026-11-04 garnish "garnish manual"
.SH NAME
gerant\-net \- setuid helper that creates network devices for garnish NAT VMs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gerant\-net nat
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR gerantd (1)
runs
.B gerant\-net
every time a VM with
.B [Network] Mode=nat
starts. It is installed setuid root, since only root can create network
devices, and is not meant to be run by hand. Standard output has to be a
Unix socket: the helper passes its results over it and exits.
.PP
It
.IP 1. 4
becomes the calling user, keeping only
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
and
.BR CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE ,
.IP 2.
opens every file and socket it will use, then installs a seccomp filter
that forbids opening anything else and limits the remaining calls to those
descriptors,
.IP 3.
creates tap
.BI garnish n
for the lowest free /30 of
.BR Subnet= :
the host gets its first address and the guest its second
.RI ( .4n+1
and
.IR .4n+2 ),
.IP 4.
gives the tap the
.IR n -th
/64 of
.BR Subnet6= ,
with the host at
.B ::1
and the guest at
.BR ::2 ,
.IP 5.
turns IPv4 and IPv6 forwarding on and loads its nftables tables, which NAT
the guests to the outside and keep them from reaching each other,
.IP 6.
binds UDP and TCP sockets to port 53 of the host's addresses on the tap,
for
.B gerantd
to answer the guest's DNS queries,
.IP 7.
sends the tap's descriptor, the DNS sockets and the addresses to the
caller.
.PP
The caller can't choose the tap's name or addresses. When IPv6 can't be set
up safely, the tap gets IPv4 only; when the DNS sockets can't be bound, the
guest gets public DNS servers. The reason goes to standard error, which
.B gerantd
logs.
.PP
The tap isn't persistent: the kernel removes it when the last descriptor is
closed, that is, when the VM stops.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on failure, 2 for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/garnish/network.conf
The subnets the taps are taken from; see
.BR garnish\-network.conf (5).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/network/10\-garnish.network
Keeps
.BR systemd\-networkd (8)
from managing the
.BI garnish n
taps.
.SH SECURITY
Anyone can run
.BR gerant\-net ,
and each tap holds one of the subnet's /30 networks until it is closed;
there is no per-user limit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gerantd (1),
.BR garnish\-network.conf (5),
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR nft (8)
